<p>Britain, France, Germany, Italy, the Netherlands and Japan said they were ready to support “appropriate efforts” to ensure commercial ships can pass safely through the Strait of Hormuz, a vital energy chokepoint. Some reports also included Canada in the joint move, which came as several governments said they would work to help stabilize energy markets after a sharp shipping disruption linked to the widening Middle East war. The statement blamed Iran for attacks on unarmed commercial vessels, strikes on civilian energy infrastructure, and the effective closure of the strait. It urged Tehran to stop laying mines, launching drone and missile attacks, and taking other steps that block maritime traffic. In peacetime, the Strait of Hormuz carries about one-fifth of global crude oil and liquefied natural gas trade, so the coordinated response signals an effort to prevent a prolonged shock to global shipping and fuel supplies.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/XxQ1Tz0aYb61rpAghC4QWfUEmm9FwQzga60QPMXwJl2RH5aUhYDivWr7ya6r9gnGjR85ApadhlmClG44yehdZ8hZg8XtMSL2dR33fIKSkOnB' alt='A graphic map shows Strait of Hormuz.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Shipping incidents: Since March 1, at least 21 commercial vessels, including 10 tankers, have been attacked or have reported incidents in the Gulf, the Strait of Hormuz, or the Gulf of Oman, according to UKMTO figures cited by Dawn.</li><li>Conflict trigger: Dawn reported that the current maritime disruption followed a war that began on Feb. 28, when the United States and Israel started bombing Iran. Tehran then retaliated against US assets and bases in the Gulf and restricted access to the strait.</li><li>Unconfirmed claims: Across all vessel types, four additional attacks claimed by Iran’s Revolutionary Guards had not been confirmed by international authorities, underscoring the fog surrounding some incidents at sea.</li><li>Geographic spread: The reported attacks and incidents have occurred across the Gulf, the Strait of Hormuz, and the Gulf of Oman, showing that risks extend beyond the narrowest part of the chokepoint to its wider shipping approaches.</li></ul><blockquote>We express our readiness to contribute to appropriate efforts to ensure safe passage through the strait. We welcome the commitment of nations that are engaging in preparatory planning. - Leaders of the United Kingdom, France, Germany, Italy, the Netherlands and Japan</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>United Kingdom, France, Germany, Italy, the Netherlands and Japan: Dawn’s version of the statement highlighted the group’s demand that Iran stop interfering with ship navigation systems and boarding vessels, not just the more visible threats from drones, missiles, and sea mines. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1983777/european-countries-japan-express-readiness-to-safeguard-shipping-through-strait-of-hormuz'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Canada and partner countries: The Globe and Mail framed the pledge as a willingness to use “appropriate efforts” to reopen Hormuz amid concern that the war could cause lasting damage to global energy supplies. (<a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/article-canada-japan-european-allies-willing-to-use-appropriate-efforts-to/'>The Globe and Mail</a>)</li><li>France, Germany and Italy: Al-Monitor reported that these three countries said any direct initiative on Hormuz would come only after a ceasefire. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/03/italy-germany-and-france-offer-help-hormuz-only-after-ceasefire'>Al-Monitor</a>)</li><li>World Socialist Web Site: WSWS described the coordinated pledge by European powers and Japan as preparation to join a wider war against Iran, a much more critical reading than the governments’ own framing. (<a href='https://www.wsws.org/en/articles/2026/03/20/ghoc-m20.html'>WSWS</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1983777/european-countries-japan-express-readiness-to-safeguard-shipping-through-strait-of-hormuz'>European countries, Japan express ‘readiness’ to safeguard shipping through Strait of Hormuz</a> - dawn.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/joint-statement-on-strait-of-hormuz-by-european-nations-japan-canada'>Joint statement on Strait of Hormuz by European nations, Japan, Canada</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/article-canada-japan-european-allies-willing-to-use-appropriate-efforts-to/'>Canada, Japan, European allies willing to use ‘appropriate efforts’ to reopen Strait of Hormuz</a> - theglobeandmail.com</li><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/six-allies-ready-to-help-efforts-to-ensure-safe-passage-through-hormuz-strait/article70764060.ece'>Six allies ready to help ‘efforts to ensure safe passage’ through Hormuz Strait</a> - thehindu.com</li><li><a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/03/italy-germany-and-france-offer-help-hormuz-only-after-ceasefire'>Italy, Germany and France offer help with Hormuz only after ceasefire</a> - al-monitor.com</li><li><a href='https://www.wsws.org/en/articles/2026/03/20/ghoc-m20.html'>European powers prepare participation in war against Iran</a> - wsws.org</li></ul>